Three $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ binding domains, 1–3, were found in MFM-127 (Fig. 3). $\mathbf{CO}_{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{1}$ is situated in an equivalent site to $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{D}_{2}–\mathbf{4}$ with a close contact to a carboxylate $\mathbf{C}^{\hat{\delta}+}\left[\mathbf{O}_{\mathbf{CO}_{2}}{\cdot\cdot\cdot}\mathbf{C}_{\mathbf{COO}}=3.96\left(6\right)\hat{\bar{\mathbf{A}}},\mathbf{\mathrm{<}}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}$ $\mathbf{O}{\cdot}{\cdot}{\cdot}\mathbf{C}=100^{\circ}]$ as well as side-on interactions with isophthalate rings $\big[\mathrm{O_{CO_{2}}}\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathrm{H}\mathrm{-C_{isophthal.}}=2.27\big(1\big)\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}\mathrm{,~}\prec\mathrm{C-H}\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathrm{O}\ =\ 135^{\circ}$ and CCO2/H–Cisophthal. ¼ 2.95(7) A˚, $<\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{H}{\boldsymbol{\cdot}}{\boldsymbol{\cdot}}\mathbf{C}\ =\ 114^{\circ}\mathbf{]}$ . $\mathbf{CO}_{2}–2$ occupies the corresponding site to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{1}$ in MFM-126. As for MFM-126, cooperative binding is exhibited between crystallographically equivalent $\mathbf{CO}_{2}–2$ molecules in cage B of MFM-127 $\left[\mathbf{C_{CO_{2}}}^{\bullet\bullet\bullet}\mathbf{O_{CO_{2}}}=3.04{\left(2\right)}\ \mathring{\mathbf{A}}\right.$ , $k\mathbf{C}{=}\mathbf{O}{\cdot}{\cdot}{\cdot}\mathbf{C}=128^{\circ}]$ . Crucially, these results reveal the key role of the amide groups on selective adsorption of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in MFM-126 as well as conrming that alkyne moieties do not act as effective adsorption sites for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in MFM127.
NPD data for $\mathrm{CD}_{4}$ -loaded MFM-127 exposed only two noteworthy sites of interaction. Both sites correspond directly to $\mathrm{CD}_{4}$ sites 1 and 2 observed in MFM-126, respectively. In MFM-127, $\mathbf{CD_{4}-1}$ resides in the hydrophobic triangular windows of cage B $\big[\mathrm{D}_{4}\mathrm{C}\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathrm{H}-\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{isophthal.}}=2.93\big(1\big)\mathring{\mathrm{A}},\thickspace\mathrm{C}\cdot\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathrm{C}=120^{\circ}\big],$ further showing that this pocket provides an optimal environment for $\mathrm{CD}_{4}$ . $\mathbf{CD}_{4}–2$ is found in the center of cage B and interacts weakly to the framework alkyne $\left[\mathrm{D}_{4}\mathrm{C}\cdots\mathrm{}\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{\underline{{{\Omega}}}}^{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}\equiv\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{framework}}=4.39(1)\mathrm{\}\mathrm{\mathring{A}}\right]$ revealing that the alkyne moiety has little effect on $\mathrm{CD}_{4}$ adsorption.
Thus, signicantly, the NPD study reveals that it is a combination of cooperative binding as well as the amide functionality that leads to enhanced interaction of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ with MFM-126. On the other hand, NPD data reveal that alkyne-functionalized MFM-127 exhibits much weaker interaction with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , but that the alkyne moieties play critical roles in acetylene binding, with over half of the adsorbed acetylene molecules exhibiting interactions $(<4\mathring\mathbf{A})$ to alkyne moieties in the pore of MFM-127. This represents the rst example of direct visualization of acetylene binding to an alkyne moiety in porous materials.
## Inelastic neutron scattering (INS) studies of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ -loaded MFM126
To gain further understanding into $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ binding in MFM-126, inelastic neutron scattering (INS) was measured as a function of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ -loading (Fig. S18†). The INS spectrum for the bare MOF exhibits numerous vibrational modes which have been assigned by comparison with a DFT-calculated INS spectrum (Fig. ${\bf S17\dagger}$ . Interestingly, unlike previously reported with isostructural MFM-136,27 MFM-126 exhibits signicant interactions with guest $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . Upon $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ loading, there is a shi in the peak at $82.8~\mathrm{meV}$ (assigned to the in-plane bending of the amide group) to $84.1~\mathrm{meV}$ signifying an interaction between adsorbed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and the amide group, such as those visualized in the structure model of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ -loaded MFM-126. This result contrasts with that of MFM-136 (ref. 27) and conrms the importance of cooperativity between functional groups (in this case amide) and the pore geometries on guest binding.
## In situ synchrotron FT-IR microspectroscopy of MFM-126
To investigate further the nature of host–guest interactions, in situ synchrotron FT-IR microspectroscopy was conducted on a single crystal of activated MFM-126. FTIR spectra were collected as a function of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ -loading by increasing the partial pressure of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in ${\bf N}_{2}$ from 0 to 1.0 bar $\left(\mathrm{ppCO}_{2}\right.$ ; Fig. 4). The $\nu_{1}+$ $\nu_{3}$ $\mathrm{;}\left(3695~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\right)$ and $2\nu_{2}+\nu_{3}$ ( $3590~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) combination bands of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ (Fig. 4a) were used to monitor $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ sorption as the fundamental antisymmetric stretch at ${\sim}2348~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ saturates at $\mathsf{p p C O}_{2}$ above 0.2 bar. These combination bands are red-shied from their free values (3714 and $3612~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively) at all partial pressures of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ loading and increase in intensity as a function of $\mathsf{p p C O}_{2}$ , conrming that $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ interacts strongly with the framework (Fig. 4a). Activated MFM-126 has a $\nu(\mathrm{N\mathrm{-}H})$ stretch at $3306~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and upon increasing $\mathsf{p p C O}_{2}$ from 0 to 1 bar, this band shis by $36~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to $3270~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . In addition, the observed shi of the amide $\scriptstyle\nu(\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{O})$ stretching band by $10\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (from $1684~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ in the absence of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to $1674~\mathrm{{cm}}^{-1}$ at 1.0 bar $\mathsf{p p C O}_{2}$ ; Fig. 4c) further indicates $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption directed by the amide group in MFM-126. Overall, these observations are highly consistent with the NPD and INS results.
## Conclusions
A series of isoreticular (3,6)-connected pyrimidyl isophthalate $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ -based MOFs, of rare eea topology have been synthesized and characterized. Although MFM-136 exhibits the highest $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake at 20 bar, it was found that upon shortening the linker unit to form MFM-126, selectivity of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake at 1 bar increases dramatically. The relative ease of synthesis of MFM-126 coupled with its high selectivity for binary mixtures of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , position this MOF as a viable candidate for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ separations. Furthermore, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ separation performance of MFM-126 has been conrmed by dynamic breakthrough experiments. NPD data of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ - loaded MFM-126 has revealed that cooperative binding of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ (at position $\mathbf{CO}_{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{1}\stackrel{\cdot}{}$ ) and binding to amide groups that decorate cage B both have strong effects on the observed selective $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ binding. The in situ spectroscopic studies using INS and FTIR also establish that adsorbed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ interacts strongly with the amide groups of MFM-126, with signicant shis of amide $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ and N–H) vibrational bands on $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ loading. Replacing the amide-group in MFM-126 with an alkyne-group to give MFM-127 leads to a decrease in both $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake capacities relative to MFM-126, but affords a $68\%$ greater $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ capacity than MFM-126. NPD experiments reveal for the rst time that acetylene interacts directly with alkyne moieties of MFM-127 in the pore. Notably, over $50\%$ of the acetylene observed within MFM-127 displays strong interactions $(<4\mathring\mathbf{A})$ with the alkyne functionality of the framework. The understanding gained in this study provides further insights into the development of materials showing improved gas binding via specic interaction to ligand sites within the MOF.
